data.content:
Since `TimeProvider` comes from DI, a `static` class can't receive it via constructor injection. You have two clean options:

### Option A — Convert to a non-static class (recommended)
Make it a normal registered service so DI can inject `TimeProvider`:

```csharp
public class TimestampFormatter(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string Now() => timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

Register it: `services.AddSingleton<TimestampFormatter>();`

### Option B — Keep it static, pass `TimeProvider` at the call site

```csharp
public static class TimestampFormatter
{
    public static string Now(TimeProvider tp) => tp.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

No DI registration needed, but every caller must supply the provider.

---

**Option A** is the standard .NET pattern — it keeps callers clean and makes the dependency explicit. Want me to apply one of these?
